const transcripts = {
  // DATA ANALYTICS - MODULE 1
  '02d0ce24-1316-464d-a482-a8fe0fc0b481': `Welcome to the Data Analytics Professional course, Lesson 1.1 — The Data Analytics Lifecycle. Data analytics is the discipline of transforming raw data into actionable insights that drive better decisions. In today's organizations, data is generated at extraordinary volume and velocity — every customer interaction, every transaction, every operational process produces data. The organizations that extract value from this data gain competitive advantages that are increasingly difficult to replicate. The data analytics lifecycle describes the sequence of stages through which data moves from raw collection to business insight. Understanding this lifecycle is essential because different stages require different skills, tools, and approaches, and effective analytics practitioners know how to navigate all of them. Stage one is business understanding — defining the question you are trying to answer or the problem you are trying to solve. This stage is often underinvested, leading to technically sophisticated analysis that answers the wrong question. Every analytics project should start with a clear problem statement: what decision will this analysis support, and what would need to be true in the data for each possible decision? Stage two is data acquisition — identifying, accessing, and extracting the data needed to answer the question. Data may come from internal databases, external APIs, third-party data providers, surveys, or web scraping. Stage three is data preparation — cleaning, transforming, and structuring the data for analysis. This is typically the most time-consuming stage, consuming 60 to 80 percent of total project time. Stage four is exploratory data analysis — understanding the data's characteristics, patterns, and anomalies through visualization and descriptive statistics. Stage five is modeling and analysis — applying statistical and machine learning techniques to extract insights. Stage six is communication — translating analytical findings into business recommendations that non-technical stakeholders can understand and act on.`,

  '96d811fe-bedf-4b85-b585-3dc9f2d981cf': `Lesson 1.2 — Data Types and Data Structures. Understanding data types and structures is foundational to every analytics task. The choice of analytical technique, the appropriate visualization, and the correct statistical test all depend on understanding what kind of data you are working with. Quantitative data represents numerical measurements and can be further divided into discrete data, which counts distinct items like the number of transactions or the number of customers, and continuous data, which measures values on a continuous scale like temperature, revenue, or height. Quantitative data supports arithmetic operations and is the basis for most statistical analyses. Qualitative data, also called categorical data, represents categories or groups. Nominal data has no inherent order — product category, customer segment, country. Ordinal data has a meaningful order but the intervals between values are not necessarily equal — customer satisfaction ratings from very dissatisfied to very satisfied, or education level from high school to graduate degree. Time series data is sequential data collected at regular time intervals — daily sales figures, hourly website traffic, monthly churn rates. Time series data has unique analytical properties because observations are correlated with previous observations, which requires specialized analytical techniques. Structured data is organized in rows and columns with predefined schema — the data in your relational database tables. It is the easiest to analyze because the organization is already done. Semi-structured data has some organizational structure but does not conform to a rigid schema — JSON files, XML documents, and email are examples. Unstructured data has no predefined format — text documents, images, audio, and video. Analyzing unstructured data requires natural language processing or computer vision techniques in addition to standard analytics methods.`,

  '2c92552d-127f-4731-9fd6-4801e4e79dcb': `Lesson 1.3 — Analytics Tools Landscape. The data analytics tools landscape is broad and evolving rapidly. Choosing the right tools for your use case — and developing proficiency in the tools most valued in your target market — is one of the most important career decisions you will make as an analytics practitioner. Let me give you an organized overview of the landscape. Programming languages form the foundation of professional data analytics. Python is the dominant language for data science and machine learning, with a rich ecosystem of libraries: pandas for data manipulation, NumPy for numerical computing, matplotlib and seaborn for visualization, scikit-learn for machine learning, and many more. Python's versatility and the breadth of its ecosystem make it the most valuable analytics language to learn. R is a statistical computing language with exceptional capabilities for statistical analysis, visualization, and academic research. It is particularly strong in fields like biostatistics, finance, and academic data science. SQL is the universal language for data extraction and manipulation from relational databases. Every analytics practitioner must be proficient in SQL — it is the most commonly required skill in analytics job descriptions. Business intelligence tools enable non-technical users to create dashboards and reports from pre-prepared data. Power BI and Tableau are the market leaders. Both support drag-and-drop dashboard creation, integration with common data sources, and sharing capabilities for broad organizational use. Data engineering tools like Apache Spark, dbt, and Airflow support the data pipelines that prepare data for analysis at scale. As you advance in your analytics career, understanding these tools becomes increasingly important. Cloud analytics platforms like Snowflake, BigQuery, and Redshift are modern data warehouses that support large-scale analytics workloads.`,

  '32dbaa96-e898-4e70-a218-7171615c0af2': `Lesson 1.4 — Working with Data Sources. Data analytics projects begin with data, and data comes from many sources with varying characteristics, quality levels, and access methods. Understanding how to identify, access, and evaluate data sources is a fundamental analytics skill. Internal data sources are the data your organization already generates through its operations. Transactional databases contain records of business events — sales transactions, customer interactions, inventory movements. CRM systems contain customer relationship data. ERP systems contain financial and operational data. Log files from applications and infrastructure contain event-level activity data. Internal data sources are typically the richest and most relevant for business analytics questions, and they are accessible without the cost and complexity of external data acquisition. External data sources augment internal data with context that the organization does not generate itself. Government statistical agencies provide demographic, economic, and social data that contextualizes business performance. Commercial data providers sell consumer behavior, market, and competitive intelligence data. Social media platforms provide APIs for accessing public conversation data. Weather data services provide historical and forecast weather information relevant to businesses whose performance is weather-sensitive. Web data can be accessed through scraping and APIs for competitive intelligence, market research, and trend analysis. Data quality evaluation is a critical step when accessing any data source. The key dimensions of data quality are completeness — are there missing values that could bias analysis? Accuracy — does the data correctly represent the real-world phenomena it measures? Consistency — are the same concepts measured the same way across different sources and time periods? Timeliness — is the data current enough for the decisions it will support? Uniqueness — are there duplicate records that would inflate counts and distort analysis?`,

  '0f06f4f2-c924-48d1-803f-da881f282b17': `Lesson 1.5 — AI Tools for Data Analytics. Artificial intelligence is transforming data analytics in two distinct ways: as a subject of analysis — organizations want to understand how AI is affecting their business — and as a tool for performing analysis more efficiently and effectively. In this lesson, we focus on the second dimension: how AI tools are changing what analytics practitioners can accomplish. AI-powered data preparation tools dramatically reduce the most time-consuming stage of analytics work. Tools like Trifacta and OpenRefine use machine learning to suggest data transformations, detect anomalies, identify duplicate records, and standardize inconsistent values. What previously required hours of manual Python or SQL scripting can now be accomplished in minutes with AI assistance. Automated exploratory data analysis tools like DataRobot and H2O.ai can generate comprehensive EDA reports including distributions, correlations, missing value analysis, and statistical summaries with a few clicks. This does not eliminate the need for analytical judgment — you still need to interpret the findings and decide what deserves deeper investigation — but it dramatically reduces the time to initial insights. Natural language querying tools allow business users to ask questions of their data in plain English without writing SQL. Tools like ThoughtSpot and Power BI's Q&A feature translate natural language questions into SQL queries and return visualizations automatically. This democratizes data access for stakeholders who cannot write code. AI-assisted code generation for analytics, powered by GitHub Copilot and ChatGPT, accelerates Python and SQL development for analytics practitioners. You describe what you want to accomplish and AI generates the code structure, which you then review and refine. The combination of these AI capabilities allows analytics practitioners to deliver insights faster while maintaining the analytical rigor and business judgment that AI cannot replace.`,

  // DATA ANALYTICS - MODULE 2
  '1c009f06-260f-4b4d-88c5-c942e6ed9484': `Welcome to Module 2, Lesson 2.1 — SQL Fundamentals. SQL, Structured Query Language, is the universal language for interacting with relational databases. It is the most commonly required technical skill in data analytics job descriptions, and proficiency in SQL is the single most important technical investment you can make early in your analytics career. Let me build your SQL foundation from the ground up. A relational database organizes data into tables, where each table represents a specific entity — customers, orders, products, transactions. Each row in the table represents one instance of that entity, and each column represents an attribute of that entity. Tables are related to each other through keys — primary keys uniquely identify each row within a table, and foreign keys reference primary keys in other tables to represent relationships between entities. The SELECT statement is the foundation of SQL data retrieval. SELECT column1, column2 FROM table WHERE condition ORDER BY column LIMIT n is the basic structure. SELECT specifies which columns to return. FROM specifies which table to query. WHERE filters rows based on conditions. ORDER BY sorts the result set. LIMIT restricts the number of rows returned. Aggregate functions summarize data across multiple rows. COUNT counts rows, SUM adds numeric values, AVG calculates averages, MIN and MAX find extreme values. Aggregate functions are used with GROUP BY to summarize data by category — for example, total sales by product category or average order value by customer segment. JOIN operations combine data from multiple tables based on their relationships. INNER JOIN returns rows where the join condition is met in both tables. LEFT JOIN returns all rows from the left table and matching rows from the right table. Understanding joins is essential because real-world analytics questions almost always require combining data from multiple tables.`,

  '91937b72-5476-4035-aebd-7b0962e67f08': `Lesson 2.2 — Advanced SQL: Window Functions and CTEs. Once you have mastered SQL fundamentals, window functions and common table expressions are the techniques that separate intermediate SQL practitioners from advanced ones. These capabilities dramatically expand what analytical questions you can answer with SQL alone, often eliminating the need to export data to Python for post-processing. Window functions perform calculations across a set of rows related to the current row, without collapsing the result set the way aggregate functions do. The OVER clause defines the window — the set of rows the function operates on. ROW_NUMBER assigns a sequential number to each row within a partition. RANK and DENSE_RANK assign rankings within a partition. LAG and LEAD access values from previous or subsequent rows, enabling period-over-period comparisons. Running totals and moving averages use SUM with a window frame specification. These capabilities make it possible to calculate year-over-year growth, rank customers by lifetime value within each region, identify the top N products in each category, or calculate a 30-day rolling average — all without leaving SQL. Common Table Expressions, or CTEs, make complex SQL queries readable and maintainable by breaking them into named, logical steps. The WITH clause introduces a CTE: WITH monthly_sales AS (SELECT month, SUM(revenue) AS total_revenue FROM orders GROUP BY month) SELECT * FROM monthly_sales WHERE total_revenue > 100000. Recursive CTEs are a powerful extension that allows a CTE to reference itself, enabling hierarchical queries — like traversing an organizational hierarchy or computing cumulative totals — that would be extremely difficult to write without recursion.`,

  'f78b2615-0133-4f2d-8fb1-b47c96b89a9a': `Lesson 2.3 — SQL for Business Analytics. The real power of SQL for data analysts is not in the technical syntax but in how it translates business questions into data queries. Let me show you how to approach the most common business analytics questions using SQL. Customer segmentation is a foundational analytics use case. A simple RFM analysis — Recency, Frequency, Monetary value — can be implemented entirely in SQL. Recency is the time since the customer's last purchase, calculated with DATEDIFF and MAX. Frequency is the count of purchases, calculated with COUNT. Monetary value is the total spend, calculated with SUM. Segmenting customers by these three dimensions reveals your most valuable customers, your at-risk customers, and your dormant customers. Cohort analysis tracks the behavior of user groups over time, typically defined by when they first engaged with your product. It is essential for understanding customer retention and lifetime value. In SQL, cohort analysis requires identifying each user's cohort date, calculating the time elapsed since cohort entry, and aggregating behavior metrics by cohort and time period. Funnel analysis measures conversion through a sequence of steps — for example, visit to product view to add to cart to checkout to purchase. Each step in the funnel is a separate condition, and the conversion rate between steps reveals where you are losing potential customers. SQL window functions with LAG and LEAD make funnel analysis efficient by tracking whether a user completed each step within a defined time window. Attribution analysis determines which marketing touchpoints contributed to conversions. First-touch, last-touch, and multi-touch attribution models each answer slightly different questions about marketing effectiveness, and all can be implemented in SQL with the right event-level data.`,

  '7bb4adda-274e-4bfd-849d-37f9b0f2077a': `Lesson 2.4 — Query Optimization for Analysts. Writing SQL queries that return correct results is necessary but not sufficient. Queries that work correctly but run slowly create bottlenecks in data pipelines, frustrate business users waiting for dashboards to load, and consume expensive compute resources. Query optimization is the skill of writing SQL that is both correct and efficient. Understanding how databases execute queries is the foundation of optimization. When you submit a SQL query, the database engine parses it, creates a query plan — a sequence of operations to retrieve the data — and executes that plan. The query optimizer tries to find the most efficient plan, but it makes decisions based on statistics about the data, and those statistics can be stale or incomplete. The EXPLAIN or EXPLAIN ANALYZE command shows you the execution plan the database chose, which is invaluable for diagnosing slow queries. Indexes are the primary tool for query optimization. An index on a column allows the database to find rows matching a condition without scanning the entire table. Without an index, a query like WHERE customer_id = 12345 requires reading every row in the table. With an index on customer_id, the database jumps directly to the matching rows. Indexes dramatically speed up queries on large tables but add storage overhead and slow down write operations. Join optimization is critical for multi-table queries. Joining large tables is expensive, and the order and method of joins significantly affects performance. Filter conditions should be applied as early as possible to reduce the size of intermediate result sets before joining. Avoid joining on non-indexed columns in large tables. Use subqueries or CTEs to pre-aggregate data before joining when possible.`,

  'fac390a4-0799-4f2c-b2cb-bbbcc33b1e38': `Lesson 2.5 — SQL in Cloud Data Warehouses. Modern data analytics is increasingly performed in cloud data warehouses — platforms designed specifically for large-scale analytical workloads. Understanding how these platforms differ from traditional relational databases and how to use their unique capabilities is essential for analysts working in modern data stacks. Snowflake, Google BigQuery, and Amazon Redshift are the three dominant cloud data warehouses. They share key characteristics that distinguish them from traditional databases: columnar storage optimizes for analytical queries that read specific columns across many rows, rather than the row-oriented storage of transactional databases; massively parallel processing distributes query execution across multiple compute nodes for large datasets; and elastic scaling allows compute resources to be increased during heavy query periods and reduced when demand is lower, managing costs effectively. Each platform has unique SQL extensions and optimization approaches. Snowflake's semi-structured data support allows querying JSON data using SQL syntax with the VARIANT data type and the colon notation for accessing nested fields. BigQuery's partitioned and clustered tables dramatically reduce query costs by limiting the data scanned to relevant partitions. Redshift's distribution styles control how data is distributed across compute nodes, and choosing the right distribution style for your query patterns is a key optimization decision. Cost management is an important consideration in cloud data warehouses because you pay for the compute and storage you use. Query optimization reduces costs by scanning less data. Materialized views and result caching avoid redundant computation. Workload management tools prioritize queries from business users who need fast results over large batch jobs that can wait.`,

  // DATA ANALYTICS - MODULE 3
  '3eb3d46c-12a0-48c4-a896-ce07c9fe0b48': `Welcome to Module 3, Lesson 3.1 — Python and Pandas Fundamentals. Python is the lingua franca of data science and analytics. Its combination of readability, versatility, and an unmatched ecosystem of analytics libraries makes it the most valuable programming language an analytics practitioner can learn. This lesson builds your Python and pandas foundation. Python basics for analytics start with data types: integers, floats, strings, booleans, lists, tuples, dictionaries, and sets. Understanding when to use each data type and how to manipulate them is fundamental. List comprehensions, a Python-specific syntax for creating lists through transformation and filtering, are both more readable and more efficient than equivalent loops and are used extensively in analytics code. Pandas is built on top of NumPy and provides the DataFrame data structure — a two-dimensional, size-mutable, potentially heterogeneous tabular data structure with labeled axes. Think of a pandas DataFrame as a powerful Excel spreadsheet that you control with code. Creating a DataFrame from a CSV file is as simple as pd.read_csv with the file path. Once loaded, you can inspect the data with .head, .info, and .describe methods that show the first rows, column types, and descriptive statistics respectively. Series are the one-dimensional equivalent of DataFrame columns and support vectorized operations — applying a function to every element simultaneously — that are much faster than Python loops for large datasets. Indexing and slicing DataFrames uses both label-based indexing with .loc and integer-position-based indexing with .iloc. Understanding the difference between these two and when to use each prevents a common source of bugs in analytics code.`,

  '6b4b08b8-87c9-4730-b209-0ce67150b282': `Lesson 3.2 — Data Cleaning with Python. Real-world data is messy. Missing values, inconsistent formatting, duplicate records, outliers, and encoding errors are the norm rather than the exception. Data cleaning is the process of detecting and correcting these problems, and it typically consumes more time than the actual analysis. Developing efficient, systematic data cleaning workflows is one of the most valuable skills an analytics practitioner can develop. Missing value handling is the first data cleaning challenge. Pandas represents missing values as NaN — not a number. The .isnull and .notnull methods identify missing values. .fillna replaces missing values with a specified value, the column mean, or a forward-filled previous value. .dropna removes rows or columns with missing values. The right approach depends on why the values are missing and how many there are. Imputing missing values with the column mean is appropriate for small percentages of random missingness. Dropping rows with missing values is appropriate when missingness is a small percentage and random. Creating an indicator variable for missingness is appropriate when the fact of being missing is itself informative. Duplicate records inflate counts and distort aggregations. .duplicated identifies duplicate rows. .drop_duplicates removes them, keeping either the first or last occurrence. Text standardization addresses inconsistent formatting in string columns — mixed case, leading and trailing whitespace, abbreviations versus full names. The .str accessor provides string methods that apply to every element in a Series: .strip removes whitespace, .lower converts to lowercase, .replace substitutes patterns. Outlier detection and handling requires judgment about whether outliers represent data errors or genuine extreme values. Statistical methods like z-score and IQR-based bounds identify statistical outliers.`,

  'f05cfa3b-9982-4f8b-a927-f9d6996832ff': `Lesson 3.3 — Data Visualization with Python. Data visualization is the art and science of representing data graphically to reveal patterns, trends, and relationships that would be invisible in tabular form. Effective visualization requires both technical skill — knowing how to create different chart types — and design judgment — knowing which chart type best communicates your specific insight to your specific audience. Python's visualization ecosystem includes matplotlib, the foundational library that all others build on; seaborn, which provides a higher-level interface for statistical visualizations with better default aesthetics; and plotly, which creates interactive visualizations for web-based reporting. Chart type selection is the most important visualization decision. Bar charts compare categorical values. Line charts show trends over time. Scatter plots show relationships between two continuous variables. Histograms show the distribution of a single continuous variable. Box plots show the distribution including median, quartiles, and outliers across categories. Heatmaps show the magnitude of values in a matrix format, often used for correlation matrices. Choosing the wrong chart type communicates the data less effectively and can mislead the audience. Design principles that separate good visualizations from bad ones include: data-ink ratio — maximize the proportion of ink devoted to data, minimize decorative elements; appropriate scale — y-axes that do not start at zero can exaggerate differences; color choices — use color purposefully to encode information, not decoratively; clear labeling — axis labels, titles, and annotations should make the chart self-explanatory; and accessibility considerations including color blindness-friendly palettes.`,

  '27754812-137c-41e3-a7fd-b604c90b3ca9': `Lesson 3.4 — Statistical Analysis with Python. Statistics is the mathematical foundation of data analytics. Understanding key statistical concepts enables you to draw valid conclusions from data, quantify uncertainty honestly, and avoid the common analytical mistakes that produce misleading results. Descriptive statistics summarize the characteristics of a dataset. Measures of central tendency — mean, median, and mode — describe the typical value. Measures of spread — variance, standard deviation, and range — describe how much values vary. Skewness measures the asymmetry of the distribution. Kurtosis measures the weight of the tails relative to a normal distribution. These statistics inform every analytical decision from data cleaning to model selection. Probability distributions describe the likelihood of different outcomes. The normal distribution is the most important in statistics because of the central limit theorem — the means of large samples are approximately normally distributed regardless of the underlying distribution. This property enables statistical inference. The Poisson distribution models count data. The binomial distribution models binary outcomes. Understanding which distribution your data follows guides the choice of appropriate statistical tests. Hypothesis testing is the framework for making data-driven decisions with quantifiable uncertainty. The null hypothesis represents the default assumption — no effect, no difference, no relationship. The alternative hypothesis represents what you are testing for. The p-value measures the probability of observing data as extreme as yours if the null hypothesis were true. Confidence intervals provide a range of plausible values for a parameter estimate. Correlation measures the strength and direction of linear relationships between two variables. The Pearson correlation coefficient ranges from negative one to positive one. Correlation does not imply causation — this warning cannot be stated too often.`,

  'dcedca3f-6184-4c87-9c4b-70e51df30a63': `Lesson 3.5 — Pandas Advanced: Merging, Reshaping, and Performance. Building on pandas fundamentals, this lesson covers the advanced capabilities that enable complex real-world analytics workflows. Merging and joining DataFrames in pandas mirrors SQL join operations. pd.merge is the primary function, supporting inner, left, right, and outer joins based on one or more key columns. The how parameter specifies the join type; the on parameter specifies the key column or columns. Understanding how to diagnose merge issues — unexpected row counts, duplicate keys, missing matches — is an essential debugging skill. Reshaping DataFrames transforms the structure of your data between wide format, where multiple measurements of the same entity are spread across columns, and long format, where each measurement is a separate row. .pivot creates a pivot table from long format to wide. .melt converts from wide to long. .stack and .unstack manipulate multi-level indexes. The appropriate shape depends on your downstream analysis requirements — most statistical models and many visualizations require long format data. GroupBy operations are the pandas equivalent of SQL's GROUP BY. The split-apply-combine pattern divides the DataFrame into groups, applies a function to each group, and combines the results. .groupby followed by an aggregation function like .sum, .mean, or .count is the basic pattern. .transform applies a function and returns a result with the same shape as the input, enabling calculations like group-wise normalization or filling missing values with the group mean. Performance optimization in pandas becomes important when working with large datasets. Vectorized operations using NumPy arrays and pandas built-in functions are dramatically faster than Python loops. Using appropriate data types — int32 instead of int64 where possible, categorical type for low-cardinality string columns — reduces memory usage and improves performance.`,

  // DATA ANALYTICS - MODULE 4
  'abfe73b6-5a3c-4590-b430-1ab0b3cc72fc': `Welcome to Module 4, Lesson 4.1 — Power BI Fundamentals. Power BI is Microsoft's business intelligence platform and one of the two dominant BI tools in the enterprise market. It enables analysts to connect to data sources, create data models, build visualizations, and publish reports and dashboards that business users can access through a web browser or mobile app. Learning Power BI opens doors to analytics roles in organizations that have standardized on the Microsoft data stack. The Power BI workflow has four stages. Data connection uses Power Query to connect to data sources — Excel files, SQL databases, cloud services, APIs, and many others. Power Query's visual interface makes it accessible to non-programmers while providing powerful data transformation capabilities. Data modeling in the Model view defines relationships between tables, creates calculated columns using DAX formulas, and builds the semantic layer that business users query. A well-designed data model makes it easy to build correct reports and hard to build incorrect ones. Visualization in the Report view creates the charts, tables, and other visuals that communicate your analysis. Power BI provides dozens of built-in visual types and supports custom visuals from the marketplace. Good dashboard design principles — clear titles, appropriate chart types, logical layout, and consistent color scheme — are as important as the technical execution. Publication and sharing distributes finished reports to colleagues through Power BI Service, Microsoft's cloud-based report sharing platform. Row-level security controls which data each user can see. Scheduled refresh keeps reports current as underlying data changes. DAX — Data Analysis Expressions — is Power BI's formula language. Mastering DAX is the most important skill for advanced Power BI development. Key DAX concepts include calculated columns, measures, and the evaluation context that determines how calculations are filtered.`,

  '757ab8ee-280c-42e7-890a-e6586f18d274': `Lesson 4.2 — Tableau Fundamentals. Tableau is Power BI's primary competitor and is particularly strong in organizations that value visual design sophistication and interactive exploration capabilities. Many data visualization practitioners consider Tableau's drag-and-drop interface the most intuitive in the industry for exploratory analysis and executive-quality dashboard creation. Tableau's core concept is the distinction between dimensions and measures. Dimensions are categorical fields used to slice and dice data — product category, region, customer segment. Measures are quantitative fields that can be aggregated — revenue, quantity, profit. Dragging dimensions and measures to rows, columns, and the marks shelf creates visualizations interactively. Tableau automatically chooses an appropriate chart type based on the data types you drag in, though you can override this choice. Calculated fields extend Tableau's analytical capabilities beyond the raw data. Table calculations perform computations relative to other values in the visualization — running totals, percent of total, period-over-period change. LOD expressions — Level of Detail expressions — allow calculations at different granularities than the visualization's current detail level, enabling powerful analyses like customer-level metrics in a region-level visualization. Tableau's strength in storytelling comes from its dashboard and story features. Dashboards combine multiple visualizations with filters, parameters, and actions into interactive analytical experiences. Stories create guided narratives by arranging dashboard views and annotating them with context and insights. Tableau Server and Tableau Cloud provide enterprise deployment capabilities for sharing, scheduling, and governing Tableau content. Tableau Prep is a companion data preparation tool that provides visual data cleaning and reshaping without code.`,

  '51278d79-4927-409b-8c5c-fad63ffea56d': `Lesson 4.3 — Dashboard Design Principles. Technical proficiency with BI tools is necessary but not sufficient for effective data communication. The design of your dashboards and reports significantly affects whether business users engage with them, understand them correctly, and act on them appropriately. Good dashboard design is a learnable skill grounded in both data visualization research and human psychology. The pre-attentive attributes are visual properties that the human brain processes before conscious attention — size, color, position, and shape. Using these attributes intentionally guides the viewer's attention to the most important information. Making the most critical metric larger, more colorful, or more prominently positioned ensures it receives the attention it deserves. The dashboard hierarchy determines the structure of the user's experience. Start with the most important summary metrics — the KPIs that answer the top-level business question. Then provide drill-down capabilities for users who want more detail. Do not put all available data on one page — force prioritization. Chart selection principles should match the data and the question. Use bar charts for comparing values across categories. Use line charts for trends over time. Use scatter plots for relationships between variables. Avoid pie charts for more than four categories — they are extremely difficult to read accurately. Never use 3D charts — the third dimension adds visual complexity without adding information. Color should be used purposefully to encode information, not decoratively. A consistent color scheme throughout the dashboard — where the same color always represents the same concept — reduces cognitive load. Diverging color scales work well for showing deviation from a reference value. Sequential scales work well for showing magnitude. Avoid using more than four or five colors in a single visualization.`,

  '6080ad4d-5e15-4e4b-9553-751b2c981352': `Lesson 4.4 — Storytelling with Data. Data storytelling is the skill of weaving together data, visualizations, and narrative to communicate insights in a way that moves audiences to understanding and action. It is arguably the most valuable skill in the modern analytics practitioner's toolkit because the most technically sophisticated analysis creates no value if it cannot be understood and acted upon by decision-makers. The three components of data storytelling are data, which provides the factual foundation and credibility; visuals, which make patterns and relationships perceptible that would be invisible in tables; and narrative, which provides context, causality, and implications that data and visuals alone cannot convey. Every data story has a structure: the setup establishes the business context and why this analysis matters; the conflict introduces the challenge, opportunity, or insight that the analysis reveals; the resolution presents the recommended action and the expected outcome. This narrative arc is more engaging and memorable than simply presenting findings in sequence. Know your audience before you design your story. An executive audience wants business implications and recommended actions first, supporting data second. An analytical audience wants to understand the methodology and see the underlying data. A technical audience wants to evaluate the analytical approach and assumptions. The same findings require fundamentally different stories for different audiences. Cole Nussbaumer Knaflic's book Storytelling with Data is the canonical reference for this skill, and I strongly recommend it to every analytics practitioner. The key practical principle: identify the single most important insight from your analysis and ensure that insight is unmistakably clear in your communication — even if the executive only reads the title and looks at one chart.`,

  '2eb5271d-a928-4635-be94-798a070a5853': `Lesson 4.5 — Self-Service Analytics and Data Democratization. The goal of self-service analytics is to enable business users — people without technical data skills — to answer their own analytical questions using pre-built tools and governed data assets, without requiring analyst support for every query. Done well, self-service analytics multiplies the impact of your analytics team by distributing analytical capability broadly. Done poorly, it creates a proliferation of inconsistent, ungoverned reports that erode trust in data. The foundation of successful self-service analytics is a well-designed semantic layer — a business-friendly data model that abstracts the complexity of underlying data structures and exposes metrics and dimensions in business terms. When a business user searches for Revenue in the semantic layer, they should find one authoritative definition, not six slightly different versions in different tables. Governed metrics — centrally defined, consistently calculated, version-controlled business metrics — are the building blocks of a trustworthy semantic layer. Tools like dbt Metrics, Looker's LookML, and AtScale provide semantic layer capabilities. Training and enablement are as important as the technology. Business users need to understand the available data, how metrics are defined, what questions each tool can and cannot answer, and how to recognize when they need analyst support versus when they can answer the question independently. A well-designed self-service analytics program includes a data catalog — an inventory of available data assets with descriptions, owners, and usage guidance — and a clear escalation path to analyst support when self-service capabilities are insufficient. Data governance establishes the policies and processes that ensure data is used consistently, accurately, and in compliance with privacy regulations across the organization.`,

  // DATA ANALYTICS MODULES 5-8 (condensed for remaining lessons)
  '0745ed2c-c9e2-49d5-84dd-fde0ad89399e': `Welcome to Module 5, Lesson 5.1 — Statistical Thinking for Analysts. Statistical thinking is a mindset for understanding variation, uncertainty, and causality in data. It is what distinguishes analysts who draw valid conclusions from data from those who inadvertently mislead themselves and their stakeholders. The fundamental principle of statistical thinking is that variation is everywhere and understanding variation is the key to understanding processes and systems. Every metric in your business — daily active users, revenue, conversion rate, customer satisfaction — has natural variation that occurs randomly around a central tendency. This natural variation is noise. Process changes, external events, and genuine business changes create signals — variation beyond what the process normally produces. The central challenge of statistical thinking is separating signal from noise. The normal distribution is the statistical foundation of most hypothesis testing because of the central limit theorem: sample means are approximately normally distributed for sufficiently large samples. This property enables us to quantify the probability that an observed difference is due to chance versus a genuine effect. Statistical significance measures whether an observed effect is likely to be real rather than due to random variation. Practical significance measures whether the effect is large enough to matter for the business. An effect can be statistically significant — very unlikely to be due to chance — but practically insignificant — too small to affect business decisions. Both dimensions matter. Correlation versus causation is the most important distinction in applied statistics. A correlation between two variables means they tend to move together. It does not mean one causes the other. Establishing causation requires either a randomized controlled experiment or a carefully designed observational study that controls for confounding variables.`,

  '6de5b865-1d8f-4123-a0b2-0db031432096': `Lesson 5.2 — A/B Testing and Experimentation. A/B testing is the gold standard methodology for measuring the causal impact of changes in digital products and marketing. By randomly assigning users to a control condition (A) and a treatment condition (B), and measuring outcomes for each group, A/B testing enables causal inference that observational analysis cannot provide. The key principle is randomization — when users are randomly assigned to conditions, the two groups are statistically equivalent on all factors except the treatment, so any difference in outcomes can be attributed causally to the treatment. Designing a valid A/B test requires careful attention to several factors. Sample size calculation determines how many users you need in each group to detect an effect of your expected magnitude with your desired statistical power. Running a test with too few users produces underpowered results that fail to detect real effects. Running longer than necessary wastes the opportunity cost of delaying decisions. The minimum detectable effect is the smallest improvement that would be practically meaningful for your business — this drives your sample size calculation. Randomization unit selection — user, session, or request — affects the independence assumption that underlies statistical validity. User-level randomization is appropriate for most experiments; session-level randomization is appropriate only when there is no meaningful carryover between sessions. Metrics selection requires choosing a primary metric that directly measures the impact of the treatment and guardrail metrics that detect unintended negative effects. Statistical analysis of A/B test results uses hypothesis testing: the null hypothesis is that the treatment has no effect, and the p-value measures the probability of observing results as extreme as yours if the null hypothesis were true.`,

  'd742ccae-4417-400b-821c-0ffd8fe9e508': `Lesson 5.3 — Probability Distributions. Probability distributions are mathematical models that describe the likelihood of different outcomes for a random variable. Understanding which distributions apply to different types of data enables you to make appropriate analytical choices, use the right statistical tests, and build better predictive models. The normal distribution is the most important distribution in statistics. It is bell-shaped, symmetric, and fully described by its mean and standard deviation. Many natural and social phenomena are approximately normally distributed. More importantly, the central limit theorem ensures that sample means follow an approximately normal distribution for large enough samples, regardless of the underlying distribution. The binomial distribution models the number of successes in a fixed number of independent trials, each with the same probability of success. It applies to conversion rates, click-through rates, and any other binary outcome measured across multiple trials. The Poisson distribution models the number of events occurring in a fixed time or space interval when events occur independently at a constant average rate. It applies to website visits per hour, customer service requests per day, and defects per unit of output. The exponential distribution models the time between events in a Poisson process — the time between website visits, customer service calls, or equipment failures. It is characterized by the memoryless property: the probability of an event in the next interval is independent of how long you have already been waiting. Understanding these distributions enables correct modeling choices: logistic regression for binary outcomes, Poisson regression for count data, survival analysis for time-to-event data.`,

  '880b95a4-bb89-4fec-98e3-9d0c736af6ac': `Lesson 5.4 — Regression Analysis. Regression analysis is one of the most widely used statistical techniques in business analytics. It models the relationship between a dependent variable and one or more independent variables, enabling prediction, causal analysis, and understanding of how factors relate to outcomes. Linear regression models the relationship between a continuous dependent variable and one or more predictors. The simple linear regression equation is Y equals beta zero plus beta one times X plus epsilon, where beta zero is the intercept, beta one is the slope, and epsilon is the error term. The coefficients are estimated by minimizing the sum of squared residuals — the differences between predicted and actual values. Multiple linear regression extends this to multiple predictors: Y equals beta zero plus beta one X1 plus beta two X2 plus ... plus epsilon. Each coefficient represents the change in Y associated with a one-unit change in that predictor, holding all other predictors constant. This ceteris paribus interpretation is what makes regression useful for understanding individual factor effects. Logistic regression models binary outcomes — churn versus not churn, conversion versus non-conversion, default versus non-default. It models the log-odds of the outcome as a linear function of predictors, transforming through the sigmoid function to produce probabilities between zero and one. Model evaluation requires appropriate metrics. For linear regression, R-squared measures the proportion of variance explained and RMSE measures prediction error. For logistic regression, accuracy, precision, recall, AUC-ROC, and confusion matrices evaluate model performance. The business context determines which metrics matter most — a churn model that identifies 90 percent of churners while incorrectly flagging 50 percent of loyal customers may be acceptable or unacceptable depending on the relative costs of false positives and false negatives.`,

  '3838417c-36f9-4702-9f73-9e675d5d5f24': `Lesson 5.5 — Time Series Analysis. Time series analysis is specialized because observations in a time series are not independent — each observation is correlated with previous observations. This temporal dependence violates the independence assumption of standard statistical methods and requires specialized techniques. Time series decomposition separates a time series into trend, seasonality, cyclicality, and residual components. Trend is the long-term direction — is the metric generally increasing, decreasing, or stable? Seasonality is regular, periodic fluctuation — weekly patterns in website traffic, annual patterns in retail sales. Cyclicality is irregular fluctuation associated with business cycles. The residual is the unexplained variation after removing the other components. Understanding these components improves forecasting by modeling each component separately. Stationarity is a key concept in time series analysis. A stationary time series has constant mean, variance, and autocorrelation structure over time. Most time series forecasting methods require stationarity. Differencing — subtracting each observation from the previous one — removes trend and is the most common approach to achieving stationarity. ARIMA models — AutoRegressive Integrated Moving Average — are the classical approach to time series forecasting. The autoregressive component models the relationship between an observation and lagged observations. The moving average component models the relationship between an observation and lagged forecast errors. The integrated component represents the degree of differencing applied to achieve stationarity. Selecting appropriate ARIMA parameters requires analyzing the autocorrelation and partial autocorrelation functions of the time series. Seasonal ARIMA — SARIMA — extends this to handle seasonal patterns. Prophet, developed by Facebook, is a modern time series forecasting tool that handles seasonality, holidays, and trend changes more intuitively than ARIMA.`,

  // DATA ANALYTICS MODULE 6
  '620e2f89-9059-499b-8a1d-a4e777bb1805': `Welcome to Module 6, Lesson 6.1 — Introduction to Machine Learning. Machine learning is the field of computer science that enables systems to learn from data and improve their performance without being explicitly programmed for each specific task. For data analysts, machine learning extends your analytical toolkit beyond description and inference into prediction and automation. Understanding the core concepts and common algorithms enables you to apply machine learning appropriately and evaluate ML solutions critically. The three types of machine learning are supervised learning, unsupervised learning, and reinforcement learning. Supervised learning trains models on labeled data — input features paired with correct output labels. The model learns to predict outputs for new inputs by generalizing from training examples. Classification predicts categorical outputs — will this customer churn? Is this transaction fraudulent? Regression predicts continuous outputs — what will this customer's lifetime value be? Unsupervised learning finds structure in unlabeled data. Clustering groups similar observations together — customer segmentation, document topic modeling. Dimensionality reduction compresses high-dimensional data into lower dimensions while preserving structure — Principal Component Analysis and t-SNE are common techniques. The machine learning pipeline is the sequence of steps from raw data to deployed model. Data collection and preparation creates the training dataset. Feature engineering transforms raw data into informative input features. Model selection and training fits a model to the training data. Model evaluation measures performance on held-out test data. Model deployment makes predictions available to business processes. The train-validation-test split is fundamental to honest model evaluation. Training data is used to fit the model. Validation data is used to tune hyperparameters and select models. Test data is used only for final evaluation and should never be used to make modeling decisions.`,

  'fb18898b-502b-4a15-8a8f-292402fc91dd': `Lesson 6.2 — Common Machine Learning Algorithms for Business. Understanding the key ML algorithms and when to use them enables you to match the right tool to your business problem. I will cover the algorithms most commonly applied in business analytics contexts. Decision trees create a flowchart-like model that splits data based on feature values to arrive at a prediction. They are highly interpretable — you can trace the path from input features to prediction and explain the logic to business stakeholders. This interpretability makes them valuable in regulated industries where model decisions must be explainable. Random forests improve on single decision trees by training many trees on random subsets of data and features, then aggregating their predictions. This ensemble approach reduces overfitting and typically produces better predictive performance than single trees. Gradient boosting methods — XGBoost, LightGBM, and CatBoost — are currently the most widely used algorithms for tabular business data because of their excellent predictive performance. They build trees sequentially, with each tree correcting the errors of the previous ones. Logistic regression, despite its name, is a classification algorithm that models the probability of class membership. Its interpretability — each feature has a coefficient that directly quantifies its relationship to the outcome — makes it valuable when understanding which factors drive predictions matters as much as predictive accuracy. K-means clustering partitions observations into K groups by minimizing within-cluster variance. Choosing K requires domain knowledge and diagnostic tools like the elbow method. Customer segmentation, anomaly detection, and recommendation systems are common business applications. Neural networks and deep learning produce state-of-the-art performance on images, text, and audio but require large datasets and are less interpretable than the algorithms above.`,

  'ecd8b223-8baf-4942-ba9e-12df201723b5': `Lesson 6.3 — Model Evaluation and Validation. Building a machine learning model is only half the work — validating that it performs reliably and as expected is equally important. Poor model evaluation leads to deploying models that appear to perform well during development but fail in production. For classification problems, the confusion matrix shows the counts of true positives, true negatives, false positives, and false negatives. From these counts, precision measures what proportion of positive predictions are correct. Recall measures what proportion of actual positives were correctly identified. The F1 score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall, balancing both dimensions. The trade-off between precision and recall depends on the business context: a fraud detection model might prioritize recall, accepting more false positives to catch more fraud; a marketing campaign targeting model might prioritize precision, accepting a lower response rate in exchange for better ROI. AUC-ROC — Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve — measures the model's ability to discriminate between classes across all probability thresholds. An AUC of 0.5 is no better than random; an AUC of 1.0 is perfect discrimination. Cross-validation is the gold standard for model evaluation when data is limited. K-fold cross-validation divides the data into K equal-sized folds, trains on K-1 folds, and evaluates on the held-out fold, repeating K times and averaging the results. This provides a more robust performance estimate than a single train-test split. Overfitting occurs when a model learns the training data too well, capturing noise as if it were signal. The telltale sign is a large gap between training and validation performance. Regularization techniques — L1 and L2 penalties, dropout in neural networks — reduce overfitting by penalizing model complexity.`,

  'be675e7d-8a0f-4bf7-9f2f-181ad42c8b11': `Lesson 6.4 — Feature Engineering. Feature engineering is the process of transforming raw data into the informative input features that machine learning algorithms use to learn. It is arguably the most important step in the ML pipeline — better features consistently produce better models than more sophisticated algorithms applied to poorly engineered features. Numerical feature transformations include scaling, which puts different-scale features on comparable scales — standard scaling subtracts the mean and divides by the standard deviation; normalization scales to the 0-1 range. Tree-based algorithms like random forests are scale-invariant, but linear models and distance-based algorithms like KNN require scaling. Log transformation reduces the right skew common in financial and count data, making distributions more symmetric and improving linear model performance. Polynomial and interaction features capture non-linear relationships by creating new features as powers of or products of existing features. Categorical feature encoding converts categorical variables into numerical representations that ML algorithms can process. One-hot encoding creates a binary column for each category value. Ordinal encoding assigns integers to ordered categories. Target encoding replaces each category value with the mean of the target variable for that category — powerful but prone to target leakage if not implemented carefully. Time-based features extracted from datetime columns often have strong predictive power. Day of week, month of year, hour of day, days since a reference event, and cyclical encodings of periodic features are all valuable. Missing value imputation as a feature engineering step can use more sophisticated approaches than simple mean imputation: KNN imputation uses similar observations, model-based imputation predicts missing values from available features.`,

  '0d109e63-5d4a-4310-a9cd-500985fedbbe': `Lesson 6.5 — AI-Powered Analytics and AutoML. AutoML — Automated Machine Learning — platforms automate the most time-consuming steps of the ML pipeline: feature engineering, algorithm selection, hyperparameter tuning, and model evaluation. They enable analytics practitioners without deep ML expertise to build competitive models quickly, and they help experienced practitioners explore the problem space more efficiently. Platforms like DataRobot, H2O AutoML, Google AutoML, and Azure AutoML evaluate dozens or hundreds of algorithm and hyperparameter combinations automatically, returning the best-performing model with performance metrics and feature importance analysis. For common business ML problems — churn prediction, lead scoring, demand forecasting, fraud detection — AutoML platforms can produce production-ready models in hours rather than weeks. The role of the analytics practitioner with AutoML shifts from model builder to problem framer, data preparer, and results interpreter. You need to understand the business problem deeply enough to define the right target variable and evaluation criteria. You need to prepare data that is informative and free of leakage. And you need to evaluate results critically — understanding whether a model that performs well on validation metrics will generalize to the production environment. AI-powered feature discovery goes beyond AutoML by using AI to identify potentially predictive features from raw data that human analysts might overlook. Tools like Featuretools for automated feature engineering from relational data can generate hundreds of candidate features from transactional data, some of which prove highly predictive. The human analyst's job is to evaluate feature importance, remove features that are predictive but not causal, and ensure feature engineering logic can be replicated in the production data pipeline.`,

  // DATA ANALYTICS MODULES 7-8
  '16cc1995-7a97-4be5-9d07-145c9c0bdfbb': `Welcome to Module 7, Lesson 7.1 — Analytics Project Management. Analytics projects have unique characteristics that require adaptation of standard project management practices. Unlike software development projects where requirements can be somewhat specified upfront, analytics projects often discover that the data does not support the original question, that the relevant features are not in the available data, or that the business question needs to be refined based on what the analysis reveals. This exploratory, iterative nature requires a flexible project management approach. The CRISP-DM framework — Cross-Industry Standard Process for Data Mining — is the most widely used analytics project methodology. Its six phases are business understanding, data understanding, data preparation, modeling, evaluation, and deployment. Crucially, CRISP-DM is iterative — findings at later stages frequently require returning to earlier stages. Data understanding often reveals that additional data is needed, sending the project back to data collection. Modeling results may reveal that the business question needs refinement. Scope management in analytics projects is particularly challenging because the exploratory nature of the work makes it difficult to scope precisely upfront. Using a timebox approach — committing to a fixed time investment rather than a fixed deliverable — is often more appropriate than traditional scope-based project management. Stakeholder communication must explicitly address the inherent uncertainty of analytics work. Communicating the difference between a hypothesis to be tested and a finding that is established is essential for managing stakeholder expectations. Analytics projects require different success metrics than traditional projects. Delivering on time and on budget is necessary but not sufficient — the analysis must also produce actionable insights that inform the business decisions it was designed to support.`,

  '4bdae2fc-4191-404b-be19-be9d4b1371c2': `Lesson 7.2 — Communicating Analytics to Leadership. The ability to communicate analytical findings clearly and compellingly to non-technical leadership is the skill that most determines whether your analytical work creates business impact. The most sophisticated analysis, communicated poorly, produces no value. Mediocre analysis, communicated brilliantly, sometimes produces more action than it deserves. Lead with the insight, not the methodology. Executives are not interested in how you cleaned the data, which algorithms you evaluated, or why you chose a particular visualization approach. They want to know what you found and what they should do about it. Start every presentation with the key finding and recommendation. Provide methodology details as appendices for those who want to validate your work. Quantify business impact in terms your audience cares about. Revenue, cost, customer retention, and market share are languages leadership speaks. Correlation coefficients and p-values are not. Translate your statistical findings into business language: this model predicts churn with 85 percent accuracy, and if we act on its top 10 percent predictions, we expect to prevent 200 customer losses per month worth approximately 1.2 million dollars annually. Use progressive disclosure to manage detail level. Start with the executive summary — one to three key findings and recommendations. Move to the supporting analysis for those who want more. Have the detailed methodology available for those who need to validate your work. Know your audience's familiarity with data and analytics and calibrate your language accordingly. Handle uncertainty honestly. Providing a range of estimates with confidence intervals is more credible than a single-point estimate that implies false precision. Executives who work with analytics regularly appreciate epistemic honesty.`,

  '32e8febc-8f0e-4b44-9269-87ceb83c442c': `Lesson 7.3 — Building an Analytics Function. As analytics professionals advance in their careers, many take on responsibility for building or scaling an analytics function within an organization. This requires not just technical skills but organizational design, team management, and stakeholder relationship capabilities. The analytics function maturity model describes the typical evolution of analytics capability in organizations. Level one is descriptive analytics — reporting what happened. Level two is diagnostic analytics — understanding why it happened. Level three is predictive analytics — forecasting what will happen. Level four is prescriptive analytics — recommending what to do. Most organizations are at levels one or two, and the journey to higher levels requires deliberate investment in data infrastructure, talent, and analytical culture. Talent strategy is the most important decision in building an analytics function. The three key roles are data engineers, who build and maintain data pipelines and infrastructure; data analysts, who answer business questions with data; and data scientists, who build predictive models and conduct sophisticated statistical analysis. Early-stage analytics teams often need full-stack analysts who can do all three, while mature teams benefit from specialization. Data governance establishes the policies, standards, and processes that ensure data is consistent, trusted, and compliant. Without governance, analytics functions produce inconsistent metrics that erode stakeholder trust — different analysts produce different answers to the same question because they use different data sources, different definitions, or different calculation methods. Organizational positioning of the analytics function matters significantly. Centralized analytics teams serve the whole organization but can become bottlenecks. Embedded analytics teams within business units are more responsive but may lack consistency and scale. A hybrid model — a centralized data engineering and governance team with embedded analysts in business units — often produces the best outcomes.`,

  'b8fb9f48-3e8a-4bc6-9767-2a339c9b9e06': `Lesson 7.4 — Analytics Ethics and Privacy. Data analytics practitioners have access to information about people — their behaviors, preferences, health conditions, financial situations, and social connections. This access creates ethical responsibilities that go beyond legal compliance. Understanding and acting on these responsibilities is part of being a professional data practitioner. Privacy by design means considering privacy implications at the beginning of every analytics project, not as an afterthought. Before collecting data, ask whether you need all of this data for this specific purpose. Before analyzing data, consider whether the analysis could reveal information that people did not intend to share. Before publishing findings, consider whether individual people could be identified from aggregate data. Data minimization — collecting only the data necessary for the specific purpose — is both a privacy principle and a legal requirement under GDPR and other data protection regulations. Purpose limitation means using data only for the purpose for which it was collected. Using customer transaction data to build a churn model is within the scope of the original collection purpose. Selling that data to third parties is not. Algorithmic fairness addresses the risk that ML models perpetuate or amplify historical biases. A model trained on historical hiring data may learn that people from certain demographic groups were less likely to be hired — not because they are less qualified, but because of historical discrimination. Deploying this model perpetuates the discrimination. Fairness-aware machine learning techniques and regular auditing of model outputs for disparate impact are essential for responsible ML deployment. Transparency means being able to explain how analytical decisions are made and providing recourse for people affected by automated decisions. This is both an ethical requirement and increasingly a legal one under regulations like the EU AI Act.`,

  '49316a2e-1a5b-4554-a7df-9780adfe8690': `Lesson 7.5 — Analytics Portfolio and Career Development. Building a successful analytics career requires intentional portfolio development, continuous learning, and strategic career positioning. The analytics field rewards practitioners who combine strong technical skills with business acumen and communication ability — a combination that is rarer than technical skill alone. Your analytics portfolio demonstrates your capabilities to potential employers more convincingly than any resume description. A strong portfolio includes three to five complete analytics projects with documentation of the business question, data sources used, analytical approach, key findings, and business recommendations. Projects that demonstrate a complete analytics workflow — from data collection through business communication — are more valuable than narrow technical demonstrations. Open source contributions to analytics tools or datasets demonstrate both technical skills and community engagement. Blog posts or talks that explain analytical concepts or share project learnings establish your credibility and visibility in the analytics community. GitHub is the primary platform for showcasing analytics code. Keep your repositories well-organized with clear README files, documented notebooks, and clean code. Kaggle competitions demonstrate ML skills against a known benchmark and provide structured projects for your portfolio. Continuous learning is essential in analytics because the tool landscape, methodologies, and best practices evolve rapidly. Formal education through courses and certifications provides structured learning. But the most effective continuous learning comes from working on challenging real-world problems, learning from more experienced practitioners, and staying connected to the analytics community through conferences, meetups, and online communities.`,

  // DATA ANALYTICS MODULE 8
  '3918a275-1895-4cff-8c66-b557285621b3': `Welcome to Module 8, Lesson 8.1 — Google Data Analytics Certificate. The Google Data Analytics Professional Certificate is one of the most recognized entry-level analytics credentials and an excellent complement to the skills covered in this course. It is offered through Coursera, takes approximately six months to complete at ten hours per week, and requires no prior experience. The certificate covers the data analytics process using Google's tool ecosystem: spreadsheets with Google Sheets, SQL with BigQuery, data visualization with Tableau and Looker Studio, and R for statistical analysis. It is particularly valuable for career changers entering analytics without a technical background because it provides structured, hands-on learning in a widely recognized format. The certificate's strengths are its structured curriculum, hands-on projects with real datasets, and Google's brand recognition with hiring managers. Its limitation is that it covers tools at an introductory level — the SQL, Python, and statistical skills covered in this course go significantly deeper. The combination of Google's certificate for structured foundational learning and this course for advanced technical depth is a powerful preparation for an analytics career. For your preparation strategy, I recommend completing the Google certificate first if you are new to analytics, then using this course to deepen your technical skills. If you already have data experience, use this course as your primary technical training and the Google certificate to validate and credential your foundational knowledge. The capstone project in the Google certificate asks you to complete a case study demonstrating the full analytics workflow. Approach it with the data storytelling principles from Module 4 and the analytical rigor from Module 5 — it will stand out from the average submission.`,

  '93856c35-fdd1-4cde-8998-9eea64ec39f7': `Lesson 8.2 — Microsoft Power BI Data Analyst PL-300. The Microsoft PL-300 certification validates your ability to use Power BI to analyze business data and create compelling reports and dashboards. It is one of the most valued BI certifications in the market, particularly in organizations using the Microsoft data stack. The exam covers four domains: preparing data — connecting to sources, transforming data in Power Query, designing data models; modeling data — creating calculated columns and measures with DAX, optimizing model performance; visualizing and analyzing data — creating appropriate visualizations, applying filters and slicers, performing advanced analytics; and deploying and maintaining assets — configuring workspaces, managing datasets, implementing row-level security. DAX is the most challenging aspect of PL-300 preparation. The key DAX concepts you must master are the evaluation context — the set of filters applied when a measure is evaluated — and the difference between row context and filter context. Time intelligence functions like TOTALYTD, SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR, and DATEADD enable the period-over-period calculations that are ubiquitous in business reporting. CALCULATE is the most powerful and most frequently used DAX function — it evaluates an expression in a modified filter context. Data modeling principles are heavily tested. Understanding star schema versus snowflake schema, many-to-many relationships, and the impacts of different relationship directions on filter propagation is essential. Preparation strategy: build multiple complete Power BI projects using real datasets, practice DAX by building complex measures, take multiple practice exams, and review the Microsoft documentation for any areas where practice exams reveal gaps.`,

  '13743a68-262f-4d47-be36-cf13a0255d69': `Lesson 8.3 — Data Analytics Job Search Strategy. The analytics job market is strong but competitive, and a strategic job search significantly improves both your chances of success and the quality of the roles you land. Let me give you a practical strategy. Understanding the analytics role landscape helps you target your search effectively. Data Analyst roles focus on SQL, visualization, and business reporting — typically requiring two to four years of experience and paying 70,000 to 120,000 dollars in the US. Business Intelligence Analyst roles focus on dashboard development and self-service analytics — similar experience and compensation. Data Scientist roles focus on ML modeling and statistical analysis — typically requiring strong Python and statistics skills and paying 100,000 to 160,000 dollars. Analytics Engineer roles focus on data modeling and dbt — a growing category that bridges analytics and data engineering. Your resume and portfolio must directly address the job description. Analytics hiring managers scan for specific tools and skills mentioned in the job posting. If the job requires Power BI, Python, and SQL, ensure all three appear prominently in your resume with specific examples of use. Generic analytics resumes get filtered out quickly. Technical interview preparation requires practicing SQL queries, Python code, and case study walkthroughs. Coding practice platforms like LeetCode and StrataScratch have analytics-specific SQL problems. Case study interviews present a business problem and ask you to walk through your analytical approach — practice articulating your problem-solving process clearly and asking clarifying questions before diving into analysis. Networking is as important as applications. Most analytics roles are filled through referrals or direct recruiter contact rather than job postings. LinkedIn, local data meetups, and online analytics communities are all networking channels worth investing in.`,

  '0104f27f-c848-4766-bd10-25a5938c26bb': `Lesson 8.4 — Advanced Analytics and Specializations. As you advance in your analytics career, developing a specialization within the broad analytics field provides differentiation and higher compensation. Let me describe the most valuable specializations and what each requires. Marketing Analytics applies analytics to marketing measurement and optimization — attribution modeling, customer lifetime value analysis, campaign ROI measurement, and audience segmentation. The marketing analytics practitioner needs strong SQL and Python skills, deep understanding of statistical attribution methods, and familiarity with marketing technology platforms like Google Analytics, Salesforce, and marketing automation tools. Financial Analytics focuses on financial planning and analysis, risk modeling, and regulatory reporting. Strong SQL and Excel skills are essential, with Python and R for more sophisticated modeling. Understanding financial statement relationships and accounting principles is required in addition to technical analytics skills. Product Analytics focuses on understanding user behavior in digital products — funnel analysis, feature adoption, retention analysis, and experimentation. This specialization requires deep understanding of event-level behavioral data, cohort analysis techniques, and A/B testing methodology. People Analytics applies analytics to human resources — workforce planning, employee retention, hiring effectiveness, and diversity analysis. This specialization requires sensitivity to privacy and fairness issues in addition to technical skills. Supply Chain Analytics focuses on inventory optimization, demand forecasting, and logistics efficiency. Operations research methods including optimization and simulation are valued in addition to standard analytics techniques. Each specialization has its own community, conferences, and certification pathways. Choosing a specialization that aligns with your industry interest and the data you find most engaging increases both your career satisfaction and your depth of expertise.`,

  '7bf36627-42a4-4ee4-b352-85ff9af558a2': `Lesson 8.5 — The Future of Data Analytics. The data analytics field is evolving rapidly, driven by AI advances, growing data volumes, and increasing organizational data maturity. Understanding where the field is heading helps you invest in skills that will remain valuable and position yourself for emerging opportunities. Generative AI is transforming data analytics workflows in real time. AI-powered text-to-SQL tools allow business users to query databases in natural language, reducing the analyst bottleneck for simple data questions. AI code assistants dramatically accelerate Python and SQL development. AI-generated data narratives turn visualizations into plain-language explanations. These changes are shifting analyst work from execution — writing queries and building charts — toward more strategic activities: defining the right questions, ensuring data quality, interpreting results in business context, and communicating insights to decision-makers. The data mesh architecture is gaining adoption in large organizations. It distributes data ownership to domain teams who are closest to the data, while providing centralized governance infrastructure. Analytics practitioners who understand data mesh principles and can work effectively in federated data environments will be well-positioned in this emerging organizational model. Real-time analytics is becoming standard in many industries. Traditional batch analytics that processes data once per day is increasingly insufficient for use cases like fraud detection, personalized recommendations, and operational monitoring. Stream processing tools like Apache Kafka and Flink, and real-time analytics platforms like ClickHouse and Druid, are growing in importance. The convergence of analytics and AI engineering is creating new roles at the intersection of data science and software engineering — MLOps engineers, data platform engineers, and analytics engineers who build the infrastructure that makes analytical insights production-ready. Developing skills that span this boundary — both analytical and engineering — creates significant career value.`,
};

async function run() {
  const ids = Object.keys(transcripts);
  console.log(`Seeding ${ids.length} Data Analytics lessons...`);
  for (const id of ids) {
    const { error } = await supabase
      .from('videos')
      .update({ translations: { en: { transcript: transcripts[id] } } })
      .eq('id', id);
    if (error) console.error('❌', id, error.message);
    else console.log('✅', id);
  }
  console.log('Done with batch 4 - Data Analytics complete.');
}
run();
